🗺️ The Big Picture First (how the whole thing fits together)
User logs in (Google via Supabase Auth)
        ↓
User types ONE query, e.g. "protein powder"
        ↓
🧠 That ONE query hits Groq (LLaMA) which REWRITES it into 
   9 different optimized queries — one per platform
        ↓
User toggles which platforms they want (Reddit, YouTube, Quora, 
   Twitter/X, Google, PlayStore, Facebook Ads, Images, Strategy)
        ↓
EACH active platform independently: 
   scrapes/fetches data → filters for quality → sends to Groq 
   AGAIN with a specialized prompt → gets back structured JSON insights
        ↓
Results render in a dashboard, per-platform styled sections
        ↓
User can "save" → whole results page gets captured as a 
   standalone HTML snapshot → uploaded to Supabase Storage

That's the skeleton. Now let's go deep on the parts that'll actually get grilled.

1️⃣ The Smart Query System — the cleverest part of this project 🧠

File: app/api/main/smartQuery.ts

This is honestly the coolest architectural decision in the whole codebase, and you should lead with this in interviews. Here's what's actually happening:

User types "protein powder" — ONE query. But Reddit search doesn't work the same way Google Images search works, and Quora questions need to be phrased as QUESTIONS, not keywords. So instead of using the same raw query everywhere (which gives garbage results), generateSpecializedQueries() sends that ONE query to Groq/LLaMA with a prompt that says: "rewrite this into 9 platform-optimized versions" — and gets back structured JSON like:

json
{
  "RedditAnalytics": "protein powder effects experience",
  "QuoraAnalysis": "Is protein powder safe to use daily?",
  "YouTubeVideos": "protein powder study research",
  ...
}

Why this matters for the interview: This is a genuinely smart pattern — using an LLM not just for the FINAL analysis, but as a query-rewriting layer upfront. This is basically prompt-chaining / multi-stage LLM pipeline design, which is exactly the kind of thing product companies want to hear you understand.

2️⃣ The Model Management System — bigger flex than it looks 🔧

File: app/api/main/models.ts

This one's slept-on. Instead of hardcoding "use llama-3.1-70b-versatile" everywhere in the code, there's a whole system where:

The model name for EACH API (RedditAnalytics, SmartQuery, etc.) is stored in a Supabase table (api_models)
Before making any Groq call, the code fetches "hey, what model should RedditAnalytics use RIGHT NOW?" from that table
There's a 5-minute in-memory cache (CACHE_DURATION) so it's not hitting Supabase on every single request
There's even functions to validate a model actually exists on Groq before letting you set it (validateGroqModel)

Why this matters: This means you could swap Reddit's analysis from llama-3.1-8b-instant to llama-3.1-70b-versatile without redeploying any code — just update a database row. That's a legit production-grade pattern (config-driven behavior instead of hardcoded), and if an interviewer asks "how would you make your AI pipeline flexible/maintainable," THIS is your answer, straight from your own code.

3️⃣ The Reddit Pipeline — let's go step by step (this is your template for ALL platforms)

File: app/api/reddit-analytics/route.ts (724 lines — the biggest, most complete file, good sign this is a solid one to know deeply)

Step 1: Auth 🔑

getRedditAccessToken() — Reddit requires OAuth. Notice it has retry logic with exponential backoff (Math.pow(2, attempt) * 1000 — waits 2s, then 4s, then 8s between retries). This is a REAL production pattern for handling flaky APIs, not something a beginner project usually has.

Step 2: Fetch raw posts 📥

fetchRedditPosts() — hits Reddit's search API with the specialized query, sorted by relevance, limited to the past year, up to 100 posts, NSFW excluded.

Step 3: Quality filtering — this is the smart part 🎯

Two functions do the heavy lifting:

hasQualityContent() — throws out stickied/locked posts, NSFW, downvoted posts, posts with barely any engagement (< 2 comments)
calculateRelevanceScore() — a custom scoring algorithm: query terms in the TITLE score higher than in the body, terms in the subreddit name add points, and there's an engagement boost using Math.log10(score) + Math.log10(comments) (log scale so viral posts don't completely dominate)

Then it sorts by score, takes the top 15, then processes the top 10 in parallel with Promise.all().

Why this matters: Scraping ANY platform gives you a ton of noise — spam, irrelevant posts, dead threads. This relevance+quality filtering is literally the difference between "garbage in, garbage out" and actually useful insights. This is a real technical decision you can defend: "Raw search results were too noisy, so I built a two-stage filter — a hard quality gate, then a weighted relevance score — before sending anything to the LLM, to keep token usage down and insight quality up."

Step 4: Fetch comments per post 💬

For each of the top 10 posts, fetchPostComments() grabs up to 20 top comments, filters out deleted/removed ones, sorts by score.

Step 5: Clean the text 🧹

cleanText() strips markdown links, URLs, bold/italic formatting, headers — because raw Reddit markdown would confuse the LLM and waste tokens.

Step 6: THE ACTUAL "sentiment analysis" — be honest about this in interviews ⚠️

Here's something important I need you to know clearly: there's no separate NLP/sentiment model (no VADER, no TextBlob, no custom-trained classifier). The "sentiment analysis," "pain points," "emotional triggers" — ALL of it comes from ONE big, carefully engineered prompt sent to Groq/LLaMA, asking it to return a specific JSON structure with sentiment scores, pain points, niche communities, etc.

This is NOT a weakness — it's actually the RIGHT engineering call for a hackathon-to-production timeline (training a custom sentiment model would take way longer and likely perform worse than a good LLM prompt). But you need to say this correctly in an interview: "Sentiment analysis and insight extraction is LLM-driven — I engineered structured prompts that ask Groq's LLaMA model to analyze the cleaned Reddit data and return quantified sentiment scores, pain points, and emotional triggers in a strict JSON schema, rather than using a separate trained NLP model." If you say "I built a custom sentiment analysis pipeline" without qualifying it, and they ask "which model, what accuracy metric, how'd you validate it," you'll get caught. Say it exactly like above and you're golden. 👌

Step 7: Parse and return 📦

The Groq response comes back, gets JSON-parsed (with a try/catch since LLMs sometimes return malformed JSON), sources get deduplicated with [...new Set(allSources)], and everything ships back to the frontend.

4️⃣ Other platforms follow the SAME shape, with variations

I checked youtubeAnalytics.ts too — same overall pattern (fetch → filter → LLM analysis), but it uses Zod schemas to validate YouTube API responses at runtime (stronger type-safety than Reddit's manual TypeScript interfaces), and uses axios instead of raw fetch. Worth knowing: not every file uses the exact same tools (fetch vs axios, Zod vs manual interfaces) — if asked "was your codebase consistent," the honest answer is "the core pattern was consistent, but different platforms used different HTTP/validation libraries depending on what fit each API best."

5️⃣ The Snapshot/Save Feature — a nice touch 📸

File: app/api/main/save.ts

When a user hits "save," this doesn't just save the raw JSON — it literally renders the current results DOM into a standalone HTML file, converts all the images shown into base64 (so the snapshot works even if original image URLs die later), wraps it in styled HTML, uploads it to Supabase Storage, and logs an entry in a search_history table with a public URL. So users get a shareable, self-contained report of their search — smart feature, and a good one to mention as "polish" work.

6️⃣ Auth Flow 🔐

lib/supabase.ts sets up the Supabase client with PKCE flow (more secure OAuth flow for browser apps). In page.tsx, handleSignIn() calls supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth({ provider: 'google' }) — so login is Google OAuth via Supabase, not a custom-built auth system. Good to know: you didn't build auth from scratch, you used Supabase's built-in OAuth — that's a normal, smart choice, not something to hide, just something to state accurately if asked.

