🏗️ Architecture Questions

Walk me through the architecture end to end.
"User logs in via Google OAuth through Supabase. They submit one query, which gets sent to Groq's LLaMA model to be rewritten into up to 9 platform-optimized queries — one each for Reddit, YouTube, Quora, Twitter, Google, PlayStore, Facebook Ads, image search, and strategic analysis. The user toggles which platforms to activate, and each active platform independently fetches raw data through its API, filters it for quality and relevance, then sends the cleaned data to Groq again with a structured prompt to extract sentiment, pain points, and marketing insights as JSON. Results render in a dashboard, and users can save a snapshot as a standalone HTML report stored in Supabase Storage."

Why rewrite the query into 9 versions instead of using the same one everywhere?
"Different platforms need different query shapes to return good results — Quora needs a question format, Reddit search works better with terms like 'issues' or 'experience' added, PlayStore only wants app names, not full sentences. Using one generic query across all of them would give noisy, irrelevant results on most platforms."

Why Next.js over React + Express?
"Next.js gives us API routes and the frontend in a single codebase — no separate backend server to deploy and manage. For a small team building fast, that's less infra overhead, plus built-in optimizations like image handling and easy Vercel deployment."

Why Supabase over Firebase?
"Supabase gives a proper relational Postgres database instead of Firestore's NoSQL model, which fits well when you want structured relationships — like search_history tied to a user_id with normal SQL querying. It also bundles Auth and file Storage in the same platform, so we didn't need to stitch together multiple services."

Monolith or decoupled platform integrations?
"Each platform (Reddit, YouTube, Quora, etc.) has its own independent API route file — they don't call each other and can fail independently. So if Reddit's API is down, YouTube and Quora results still come through fine. It's a monolithic deployment, but the integrations themselves are decoupled."

🔍 Data Fetching / Scraping Questions

APIs or scraping?
"Official APIs where available — Reddit's OAuth API, YouTube Data API. We're not doing raw HTML scraping, which means fewer breakages from layout changes and cleaner structured data."

What if Reddit rate-limits mid-request?
"The Reddit auth function has retry logic with exponential backoff — it retries up to 3 times, waiting 2, then 4, then 8 seconds between attempts before giving up."

What if a platform's response structure changes?
"Honestly, that's a real risk — the code does have some defensive checks, like handling multiple possible Reddit response shapes, but a big structural change on their end could break parsing. This is one of the fragile points I'd flag as technical debt."

How do you avoid NSFW/spam/low quality content?
"There's a quality gate before anything reaches the LLM — it filters out stickied or locked posts, NSFW-flagged content, heavily downvoted posts, and posts with barely any engagement, like fewer than 2 comments."

Walk me through the relevance scoring algorithm.
"Query terms found in the post title score highest, especially near the start. Terms found in the body content add points too, capped so one term repeated 50 times doesn't dominate. Terms matching the subreddit name add a smaller boost. On top of that, there's an engagement score using a log scale of the post's score and comment count — log scale so a viral post with 10,000 upvotes doesn't completely drown out smaller but genuinely relevant discussions."

Why cap at top 10 posts?
"Balancing insight quality against LLM token cost and response time — processing every result would blow up both the Groq API bill and response latency for not much extra insight value, since posts past the top 15 by relevance score tend to be much less useful anyway."

🤖 AI / LLM Integration Questions

Is sentiment analysis a trained model or something else?
"It's LLM-driven, not a separate trained sentiment classifier. We send the cleaned, filtered post and comment data to Groq's LLaMA model with a structured prompt asking it to return sentiment scores, pain points, and emotional triggers in a strict JSON schema — rather than running the text through something like a fine-tuned BERT model or a rules-based sentiment library."

Why Groq/LLaMA over OpenAI GPT?
"Groq's main advantage is inference speed — their custom hardware serves LLaMA models extremely fast compared to typical API latency, which matters a lot when you're making several LLM calls per search across multiple platforms in near real-time." (This is a genuinely fair, factual answer about why teams choose Groq — you can say this confidently.)

How do you ensure valid, parseable JSON from the LLM every time?
"Two layers — the prompt explicitly requests response_format: json_object with Groq's API, which constrains the model's output to valid JSON, and the code also wraps the parsing in a try/catch with a regex fallback that strips markdown code fences in case the model wraps its JSON in backticks anyway."

What if the LLM doesn't return valid JSON?
"It's caught, logged, and returns a clear error response rather than crashing or returning garbage to the user."

Temperature settings?
"Query generation uses a low temperature, around 0.1, because we want consistent, predictable query rewrites, not creative variation. The analysis step uses a slightly higher temperature, around 0.3, giving the model a bit more room to synthesize nuanced insights while still staying mostly deterministic."

Could the LLM hallucinate pain points that aren't really there?
"Yes, that's a real risk with any LLM-based analysis — we mitigate it partially by requiring the model to cite source URLs for its findings, so insights are at least traceable back to specific posts, but we don't have a separate verification step confirming the LLM's claims against the raw text." (Good honest answer — shows awareness of a real limitation.)

Why build a system to dynamically swap the LLM model per feature?
"So we could tune cost vs quality per feature without redeploying — for example, using a smaller, faster model for simple query rewriting, and a larger model for the more nuanced sentiment analysis, and adjust that from a database config rather than changing code."

Fallback if Groq is down?
"Currently the code returns an error response to the user rather than a) queuing for retry or b) falling back to a secondary provider — that's a gap I'd flag as an area for improvement."

🗄️ Database / Backend Questions

What's your schema?
"Two main tables I can speak to: api_models, which stores which LLM model powers each feature — columns like api_name and model_name — and search_history, which logs each user's past searches with the query, the specialized queries generated, which platforms were active, and a URL to their saved snapshot."

Why cache the model lookup instead of querying every time?
"Every search could trigger several Groq calls, and each one checking Supabase first would add unnecessary DB round-trips and latency. A short in-memory cache avoids that."

Why 5 minutes specifically?
"It's a balance — long enough to meaningfully cut down repeated DB calls during active usage, short enough that if someone updates the model config, it takes effect fairly quickly without needing a restart." (Honest framing — this is a reasonable default, not some deeply calculated number, and it's fine to say that.)

Row Level Security — is user data isolated?
⚠️ This one I genuinely can't answer for you — RLS is configured in the Supabase dashboard, not visible in this codebase. You need to actually check this, because if RLS isn't enabled on search_history, any authenticated user could potentially read everyone else's search history through the anon key. This is worth verifying before your interview — I'll walk you through how to check if you want.

🔐 Auth / Security Questions

How does login work?
"Google OAuth through Supabase Auth, using the PKCE flow — Proof Key for Code Exchange — which is the more secure OAuth flow recommended for browser-based apps, since it protects against authorization code interception attacks compared to the older implicit flow."

Are API keys exposed to the client?
⚠️ This is a real, honest flag, not a made-up flaw: several API keys in the code are prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_ — the Groq key, Reddit client ID/secret, Supabase anon key. In Next.js, anything prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ gets bundled into the CLIENT-side JavaScript, meaning it's visible in the browser. The Supabase anon key is designed to be public (that's normal, RLS is supposed to protect the data behind it). But the Groq API key and Reddit client secret being NEXT_PUBLIC_ is a real security concern — anyone could extract those from the browser bundle and burn through your API quota or impersonate your app. If asked, be straight: "That's actually something I'd flag as a real issue in this codebase — several third-party API keys are exposed client-side rather than being called through a server-only route, which is a security gap I'd prioritize fixing." This is a GREAT answer because it shows you can critically evaluate code, even your own team's.

⚡ Performance / Scalability Questions

What breaks first with 1000 concurrent users?
"Almost certainly the Groq API rate limits and cost, since every search triggers multiple LLM calls — one for query generation plus one per active platform for analysis. Reddit and YouTube's own API rate limits would be the next bottleneck."

Why Promise.all() for parallel comment fetching?
"Fetching comments for 10 posts sequentially would mean 10 round-trip network calls stacked one after another. Running them in parallel with Promise.all cuts that down to roughly the time of the single slowest request instead of the sum of all of them."

How would you reduce LLM costs at scale?
"A caching layer for identical or near-identical queries within a time window, so repeated searches don't re-trigger the full pipeline; possibly a smaller/cheaper model for simpler platforms and reserving the larger model for the most complex analysis."

Base64 images in snapshot HTML — doesn't that get huge?
"Yes, that's a real limitation — base64 encoding inflates file size roughly 33% over the original, and embedding many images directly in one HTML file could get unwieldy for image-heavy searches. Storing images separately in Supabase Storage and linking to them would scale better."

🐛 Error Handling / Edge Cases

Zero relevant results from a platform?
"Returns a clear 404-style error message suggesting broader search terms, rather than silently returning empty or crashing."

LLM timeout or malformed JSON?
"Wrapped in try/catch — parse failures are logged and return an error response to the frontend instead of showing broken data."

Deleted/removed comments?
"Filtered out explicitly — the code checks comment bodies for '[deleted]' and '[removed]' markers and excludes them before sending anything to the LLM."

